  "title": "Ethical Security Awareness Testing",
  "content": "Security awareness testing is an authorized exercise that helps people practice recognizing and reporting suspicious activity. It should improve defenses without turning employees, volunteers, or visitors into real victims. Tools that imitate phishing are dual-use: authorization, scope, data handling, and safeguards determine whether their use is responsible.\n\n<b>Set the boundary before testing</b>\n\nA test needs written approval, a defined audience and time window, agreed delivery channels, and named contacts who can stop the exercise. The plan should specify what data may be collected, how long it will be retained, who may see it, and how unexpected events will be handled. Real passwords, one-time codes, payment details, or unnecessary personal information should never be collected. Testing physical access, personal devices, or third parties requires explicit additional authorization.\n\nUse the least harmful simulation that answers the learning question. A realistic message does not need a working credential trap. Landing pages can record a safe training event and immediately explain the warning signs. Controls should prevent messages from escaping the approved audience and provide a rapid way to respond if someone reports distress or a real attack appears during the exercise.\n\n<b>Measure systems, not shame</b>\n\nUseful measures include reporting rate, time to report, repeated confusion points, and whether people used the correct channel. A click rate alone does not explain why an action occurred. Results should guide clearer procedures, better technical controls, and focused education. Public rankings, humiliation, and hidden punishment discourage future reporting and distort the data.\n\nAfter the exercise, explain what was tested, identify the cues, provide a safe reporting route, and separate simulation records from real incident evidence. Share only the detail necessary for improvement. A successful program leaves participants more confident, makes reporting easier, and produces concrete defensive changes rather than a list of people who were fooled.",
  "links": [
    "Gophish and OhPhish",
    "sanctioned awareness report"
  ]
}
